%I A402224 #10 Nov 22 2026 20:10:24
%S A402224 1,4,8,135,19478,79750,6826
%N A402224 Number of sets of clues, of minimum size, that define a Numbrix puzzle on an n X n grid, up to rotations and reflections.
%C A402224 In an n X n Numbrix puzzle, you write the numbers 1, 2, ..., n^2 in the cells so that consecutive numbers are always in adjacent cells, horizontally or vertically.
%C A402224 A clue is a number already written in a cell. A set of clues defines the puzzle if there is only one solution that contains them.
%C A402224 The minimum size is the term T(n,n) from A400942: 0, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3 for n = 1, ..., 7. Thus, for n = 2, 3, 4, pairs of clues are counted, and for n = 5, 6, 7, triples are counted.
%C A402224 a(1) = 1 because in a 1 X 1 grid the minimum size is 0, and there is only one set of 0 clues: the empty set.
%C A402224 Two sets that differ by a rotation or a reflection of the grid are counted only once. The inversion k -> n^2 + 1 - k is not used.
%C A402224 For n >= 2, each class contains precisely 8 sets; consequently, the total number of sets is 8*a(n).
%H A402224 Mary Grace Hanson and David A. Nash, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.09389">Minimal and maximal Numbrix puzzles</a>, arXiv:1706.09389 [math.CO], 2017.
%H A402224 Emanuele Rizzan, <a href="https://github.com/clavis-systems/numbrix-minimum-clues">numbrix-minimum-clues</a>, GitHub, 2026.
%e A402224 a(2) = 4: the 2 X 2 grid has 8 solutions, and in each one, 4 of the 6 pairs of clues define the puzzle (specifically, those corresponding to adjacent cells). These 32 pairs form 4 classes, up to rotations and reflections.
%Y A402224 Cf. A400942 (gives minimum size), A096969.
%K A402224 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402224 1,2
%A A402224 _Emanuele Rizzan_, Nov 15 2026
